 are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040652/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7396538f8f1f0b2bdd74e07b30da8954a002935ee83ef407b4fd5e2a25132f1" "basis" 1456 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-97361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-2)q(-2)+p(0)q(0)+p(2)q(2) \\\\  & =(1+4)(2-4k)+(1)(2)+(1-4)(2-4k) \\\\  & =(10-20k)+2+(-6+12k) \\\\  & =6-8k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 6-8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =6 \\\\ k & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040652/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7396538f8f1f0b2bdd74e07b30da8954a002935ee83ef407b4fd5e2a25132f1" "basis" 1456 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-97361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040652/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28772962f48fd527a4a6fc12b1d4e3eb292fab7e2e3f74e14c0ac6ea8b4a19b3" "basis" 1456 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-97361" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040652/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7396538f8f1f0b2bdd74e07b30da8954a002935ee83ef407b4fd5e2a25132f1" "basis" 1456 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-97361" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040652/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7396538f8f1f0b2bdd74e07b30da8954a002935ee83ef407b4fd5e2a25132f1" "basis" 1456 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-97361" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040652/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7396538f8f1f0b2bdd74e07b30da8954a002935ee83ef407b4fd5e2a25132f1" "basis" 1456 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-97361" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040652/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7396538f8f1f0b2bdd74e07b30da8954a002935ee83ef407b4fd5e2a25132f1" "basis" 1456 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-97361" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040652/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7396538f8f1f0b2bdd74e07b30da8954a002935ee83ef407b4fd5e2a25132f1" "basis" 1456 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-97361" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{4}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040652/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7396538f8f1f0b2bdd74e07b30da8954a002935ee83ef407b4fd5e2a25132f1" "basis" 1456 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040652/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-43981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2\\pi $\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-43981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }+{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi ))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\pi +\\pi )+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-43981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "96b34a41f55f78fabc060dd0716c3ca8d1efc23c0908ba8969ec0517da0cdb5a"} {"question" "q-43981" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-43981" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-43981" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-97361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(-2)y(-2)+x(0)y(0)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=1-2t$ and $q(t)=2-k{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-97361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-2)q(-2)+p(0)q(0)+p(2)q(2) \\\\  & =(1+4)(2-4k)+(1)(2)+(1-4)(2-4k) \\\\  & =(10-20k)+2+(-6+12k) \\\\  & =6-8k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 6-8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =6 \\\\ k & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-97361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "96b34a41f55f78fabc060dd0716c3ca8d1efc23c0908ba8969ec0517da0cdb5a"} {"question" "q-97361" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-97361" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-97361" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-97442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)={t}^{2}$ and $y(t)=k+3t$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-97442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}(k+3t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(k{t}^{2}+3{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{k{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{3{t}^{4}}{4}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{3}+\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{3}+\\frac{3}{4} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{3} & =-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ k & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-97442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "96b34a41f55f78fabc060dd0716c3ca8d1efc23c0908ba8969ec0517da0cdb5a"} {"question" "q-97442" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-97442" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "-\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{9}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-97442" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{9}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-97449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ and $g(t)=1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\pi $\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-97449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={[-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}{2}|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-97449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "96b34a41f55f78fabc060dd0716c3ca8d1efc23c0908ba8969ec0517da0cdb5a"} {"question" "q-97449" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-97449" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-97449" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-140265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(-1)y(-1)+x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=1+{t}^{2}$ and $q(t)=-5{t}^{2}+2t+k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-140265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(2)(k-7)+(1)(k)+(2)(k-3) \\\\  & =2k-14+k+2k-6 \\\\  & =5k-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 5k-20 & =0 \\\\ 5k & =20 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-140265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "96b34a41f55f78fabc060dd0716c3ca8d1efc23c0908ba8969ec0517da0cdb5a"} {"question" "q-140265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070223/state.json" "file_sha256" "2768c1c40bd5dcff78a665854c5a918d1247c55c3dd4a3233de7c6fdfcd3a27e"} {"question" "q-140265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Acade